<h1>Assets</h1>

<h2>Storage convention</h2>

<p>Every HD document with binary assets has its own folder under a centralized <code>.hd/</code> directory at the workspace root. The folder hierarchy mirrors the document's path; the leaf folder is named with the document's base62 id:</p>

<pre><code>workspace/
├── docs/
│   └── architecture/
│       └── overview.hd     ← id: 7gXk2L9pM4nQ8vR1cT3bDe
└── .hd/
    └── docs/
        └── architecture/
            └── 7gXk2L9pM4nQ8vR1cT3bDe/
                ├── image-1.png
                └── diagram.svg</code></pre>

<h2>How assets are referenced inside a document</h2>

<p>Inside the <code>.hd</code> file, assets are referenced by bare filename:</p>

<pre><code>&lt;img src="image-1.png" alt="Architecture overview"&gt;</code></pre>

<p>The editor and any future serve-time renderer resolve bare filenames against the document's asset folder. On save, image <code>src</code> attributes are normalized back to bare filenames so the file stays portable and the on-disk source stays clean.</p>

<h2>What happens on common operations</h2>

<table border="1" cellpadding="6" cellspacing="0">
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Operation</th><th>What the editor does</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Paste an image (clipboard or drag-drop)</td>
      <td>Saves the bytes to the document's asset folder as <code>image-N.&lt;ext&gt;</code>; inserts an <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> tag with the bare filename at the cursor.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Rename a <code>.hd</code> file</td>
      <td>Moves the asset folder to match the new path. The document's <code>id</code> doesn't change, so internal references stay valid.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Move a <code>.hd</code> file to a different directory</td>
      <td>Same as rename: asset folder follows the document.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Duplicate a <code>.hd</code> file via the file explorer</td>
      <td>The copy gets a fresh <code>id</code> on first open in the editor; a new (empty) asset folder is created the first time you add an asset. The original's assets stay with the original.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Delete a <code>.hd</code> file outside the editor</td>
      <td>The asset folder is orphaned. A "garbage-collect orphaned assets" command is planned.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Why folders use the id instead of the filename</h2>

<p>If asset folders were named after the document file (e.g., <code>.hd/docs/overview/</code>), renaming the document would either break asset references or require coordinated folder renames. Keying the folder by the document's <code>id</code> decouples the asset location from the filename, so renames and moves are free at the file-system level and asset references inside the document stay valid without rewrites.</p>

<h2>Cross-document asset references</h2>

<p>The base implementation expects each document to own its assets. Cross-document references (Document A linking to an image in Document B's asset folder) are not part of the current spec; if shared assets become a recurring need, a <code>.hd/_shared/</code> convention is the likely answer.</p>
